WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.038
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4529.

2
00:00:01.038 --> 00:00:02.077
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:03.808
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Alright this is Jamie Digilormo.

4
00:00:03.808 --> 00:00:05.192
I'm here interviewing Ms. . . .

5
00:00:05.192 --> 00:00:05.538
<v BARBARA PAYNE>Barbara.

6
00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:09.000
<v DIGILORMO>. . . Barbara Payne here at her house on Charles Street.

7
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.143
<v PAYNE>Charles Avenue.

8
00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.286
<v DIGILORMO>Charles Avenue.

9
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:13.000
<v PAYNE>In Mossville, Louisiana.

10
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.714
<v DIGILORMO>In Mossville, Louisiana.

11
00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:20.429
It is a hot and bright Wednesday morning September thirtieth.

12
00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:31.286
And yeah, so we'll go ahead and get started with some basic questions about your childhood, things like that.

13
00:00:31.286 --> 00:00:37.571
So if you could . . . You've lived here your whole life, correct?

14
00:00:37.571 --> 00:00:38.143
<v PAYNE>Yes.

15
00:00:38.143 --> 00:00:38.714
<v DIGILORMO>Alright.

16
00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:45.000
Do you remember your earliest memory of living in Mossville?

17
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.844
<v PAYNE>Yeah, I guess my earliest memory probably would have been about five years old.

18
00:00:51.844 --> 00:00:53.800
About five years old.

19
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.200
The roads wasn't even paved out here or anything.

20
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:08.467
It was just straight up gravel and all the kids . . . I remember my mom, my dad, my sisters, all of that.

21
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:11.400
Well we was a happy family.

22
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:11.889
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

23
00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:15.311
<v PAYNE>You know, we had a little house.

24
00:01:15.311 --> 00:01:23.133
Maybe the house we was raised in had maybe two bedrooms, a living room, a kitchen.

25
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:23.622
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

26
00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:25.578
<v PAYNE>And that was it.

27
00:01:25.578 --> 00:01:29.000
<v DIGILORMO>And your parents and thirteen children?

28
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.071
<v PAYNE>And thirteen children.

29
00:01:30.071 --> 00:01:30.429
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

30
00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:32.571
<v PAYNE>We lived out of that house.

31
00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:34.714
<v DIGILORMO>Now what year were you born?

32
00:01:34.714 --> 00:01:35.071
<v PAYNE>‘57.

33
00:01:35.071 --> 00:01:35.786
<v DIGILORMO>Okay, ’57.

34
00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:42.214
So I was trying to think of the gap between you and your oldest . . . Is that Mr. Joseph?

35
00:01:42.214 --> 00:01:44.000
He's your oldest brother?

36
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:44.353
<v PAYNE>No.

37
00:01:44.353 --> 00:01:45.059
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay.

38
00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:47.529
<v PAYNE>I have an older brother named Alvin.

39
00:01:47.529 --> 00:01:47.882
<v DIGILORMO>Alvin.

40
00:01:47.882 --> 00:01:48.235
Okay.

41
00:01:48.235 --> 00:01:50.000
<v PAYNE>He was the oldest child.

42
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:50.353
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

43
00:01:50.353 --> 00:01:56.000
<v PAYNE>And then Ella May, the one I was telling you about, she's two years younger.

44
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:56.412
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

45
00:01:56.412 --> 00:02:00.941
<v PAYNE>And then there's another one that's two years younger than that.

46
00:02:00.941 --> 00:02:05.059
Then all the other ten are a year apart. [laughs]

47
00:02:05.059 --> 00:02:05.471
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

48
00:02:05.471 --> 00:02:07.529
So how . . . Mr. Joseph is . . .

49
00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:07.941
<v PAYNE>Sixty-eight.

50
00:02:07.941 --> 00:02:08.353
Sixty-nine?

51
00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:08.765
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

52
00:02:08.765 --> 00:02:10.412
I think you're right.

53
00:02:10.412 --> 00:02:15.765
I'm recalling . . . Okay, so there's quite a bit of age gap right there?

54
00:02:15.765 --> 00:02:18.647
<v PAYNE>Yeah because nobody knew I had sisters.

55
00:02:18.647 --> 00:02:22.765
All my sisters are that much older than I am.

56
00:02:22.765 --> 00:02:24.000
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay.

57
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.241
<v PAYNE>So between . . . I have three sisters, and between the third sister and me it was five boys.

58
00:02:30.241 --> 00:02:34.646
So I basically grew up with the boys instead of my sisters.

59
00:02:34.646 --> 00:02:35.013
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

60
00:02:35.013 --> 00:02:39.418
<v PAYNE>When I was like thirteen my sisters was all grown, gone, married.

61
00:02:39.418 --> 00:02:39.785
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

62
00:02:39.785 --> 00:02:40.152
Right.

63
00:02:40.152 --> 00:02:41.253
Out the house.

64
00:02:41.253 --> 00:02:44.190
<v PAYNE>So I was raised with all the boys.

65
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.127
<v DIGILORMO>So that's what it was like for you.

66
00:02:47.127 --> 00:02:53.000
My next question was what was it like being the baby of thirteen kids, but . . .

67
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.750
<v PAYNE>Oh they think I had the advantage [laughs].

68
00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:03.656
But when you raised with five boys and you the girl of those . . . the baby with those five boys, yeah it was rough.

69
00:03:03.656 --> 00:03:06.062
I had to fight my way through . . .

70
00:03:06.062 --> 00:03:07.094
<v DIGILORMO>I bet so.

71
00:03:07.094 --> 00:03:09.844
<v PAYNE>. . . to stand my own ground with my brothers.

72
00:03:09.844 --> 00:03:17.750
And if I didn't fight, they made me fight to make me tough on the street when I'm hanging out in the neighborhood.

73
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:18.094
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

74
00:03:18.094 --> 00:03:19.812
<v PAYNE>So I was a fighter.

75
00:03:19.812 --> 00:03:23.594
<v DIGILORMO>But would they protect you now if somebody messed with you?

76
00:03:23.594 --> 00:03:24.969
Or would they jump . . .?

77
00:03:24.969 --> 00:03:25.312
<v PAYNE>Yes.

78
00:03:25.312 --> 00:03:26.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

79
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:26.371
<v PAYNE>Yes.

80
00:03:26.371 --> 00:03:27.857
They would protect me.

81
00:03:27.857 --> 00:03:33.800
But just like they said, they trained me well so they don't worry about me. [laughs]

82
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.171
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

83
00:03:34.171 --> 00:03:34.543
Right.

84
00:03:34.543 --> 00:03:37.143
<v PAYNE>I know I hold my own ground.

85
00:03:37.143 --> 00:03:37.514
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

86
00:03:37.514 --> 00:03:37.886
<v PAYNE>But . . .

87
00:03:37.886 --> 00:03:39.000
<v DIGILORMO>That's excellent.

88
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.765
<v PAYNE>I had a baby brother . . . well one older than me.

89
00:03:42.765 --> 00:03:44.647
He died of brain cancer.

90
00:03:44.647 --> 00:03:46.529
<v DIGILORMO>I'm sorry to hear that.

91
00:03:46.529 --> 00:03:46.906
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

92
00:03:46.906 --> 00:03:50.671
And him and I, we had . . . we fought the most.

93
00:03:50.671 --> 00:03:54.812
We fought the most growing up because it was a rivalry.

94
00:03:54.812 --> 00:03:58.576
He was the baby boy and I'm the baby girl.

95
00:03:58.576 --> 00:04:02.341
So we both was fighting for our baby ground. [laughs]

96
00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:02.718
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

97
00:04:02.718 --> 00:04:03.094
Yeah.

98
00:04:03.094 --> 00:04:03.471
Right.

99
00:04:03.471 --> 00:04:04.224
That title.

100
00:04:04.224 --> 00:04:04.600
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

101
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:11.000
<v DIGILORMO>And did your parents treat you any differently you feel, since you were the baby girl?

102
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:11.440
<v PAYNE>No.

103
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.200
<v DIGILORMO>No. They were pretty . . .?

104
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.840
<v PAYNE>My mom treated everybody the same.

105
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:16.280
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

106
00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.680
<v PAYNE>And she was a single mother most of those years.

107
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.000
<v DIGILORMO>Oh really?

108
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:22.405
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

109
00:04:22.405 --> 00:04:25.238
My dad left when I was six.

110
00:04:25.238 --> 00:04:26.048
<v DIGILORMO>Oh wow.

111
00:04:26.048 --> 00:04:29.286
<v PAYNE>Out of the thirteen, and I'm the baby.

112
00:04:29.286 --> 00:04:30.905
My daddy left home.

113
00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:32.929
That might be restricted later.

114
00:04:32.929 --> 00:04:34.548
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah we'll restrict that.

115
00:04:34.548 --> 00:04:38.190
<v PAYNE>But he left home, but he was always around.

116
00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

117
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.120
<v PAYNE>My mother never had to go do anything with us without him being close enough to help.

118
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.160
So it's pretty much he wasn't at home, but he still was our dad.

119
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:50.520
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

120
00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.760
<v PAYNE>And he still did everything a daddy would do.

121
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.920
<v DIGILORMO>A big part of your life.

122
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:56.280
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

123
00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.360
Our whole lives.

124
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.600
And he came back when I graduated in 1977.

125
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:00.960
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

126
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.000
<v PAYNE>He left when I was six and he came back when I graduated.

127
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:06.412
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

128
00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:09.294
<v PAYNE>And they lived happily ever after. [laughs]

129
00:05:09.294 --> 00:05:15.059
<v DIGILORMO>Sometimes you just need a break I guess. [laughs] That's an awesome story though.

130
00:05:15.059 --> 00:05:15.471
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

131
00:05:15.471 --> 00:05:15.882
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

132
00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:17.118
Man, that's great.

133
00:05:17.118 --> 00:05:27.824
<v PAYNE>So my mom took care of us and I remember times when it was cold and a utility man would come to the house and say,

134
00:05:27.824 --> 00:05:30.706
"Ms. Payne you didn't pay your bill."

135
00:05:30.706 --> 00:05:33.176
Our bills didn’t get turned off.

136
00:05:33.176 --> 00:05:34.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

137
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:41.529
<v PAYNE>Because just like she told him, "My kids are not going to be cold, and my kids are not going to do without anything."

138
00:05:41.529 --> 00:05:41.843
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

139
00:05:41.843 --> 00:05:44.980
<v PAYNE>So she made sure that everything was taken care of.

140
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:45.922
<v DIGILORMO>Sounds like a . . .

141
00:05:45.922 --> 00:05:48.118
<v PAYNE>I had a fantastic mother and father.

142
00:05:48.118 --> 00:05:50.000
<v DIGILORMO>That’s what it sounds like.

143
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.059
<v PAYNE>We had a wonderful life.

144
00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:52.471
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

145
00:05:52.471 --> 00:05:53.706
<v PAYNE>Our whole family.

146
00:05:53.706 --> 00:05:59.471
<v DIGILORMO>So what . . . Your mom was she working while your father was out the house?

147
00:05:59.471 --> 00:06:00.706
<v PAYNE>Yeah, doing housekeeping . . .

148
00:06:00.706 --> 00:06:01.118
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

149
00:06:01.118 --> 00:06:04.000
<v PAYNE>. . . and taking care of different families.

150
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:04.442
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

151
00:06:04.442 --> 00:06:07.093
Families in Mossville or other areas?

152
00:06:07.093 --> 00:06:07.977
<v PAYNE>Other areas.

153
00:06:07.977 --> 00:06:08.419
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

154
00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:13.279
<v PAYNE>She was like the housekeeper to raising, you know, different families.

155
00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:13.721
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

156
00:06:13.721 --> 00:06:23.000
So what kind of values do you feel that your parents . . . both of your parents instilled in you growing up?

157
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:23.918
<v PAYNE>Don't steal.

158
00:06:23.918 --> 00:06:24.835
Don't kill.

159
00:06:24.835 --> 00:06:25.753
Don't lie.

160
00:06:25.753 --> 00:06:29.882
If you going to lie, you going to steal.

161
00:06:29.882 --> 00:06:30.341
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

162
00:06:30.341 --> 00:06:31.718
<v PAYNE>And respect yourself.

163
00:06:31.718 --> 00:06:36.765
And in order to get respect you have to earn respect.

164
00:06:36.765 --> 00:06:39.518
So that was a ground rule.

165
00:06:39.518 --> 00:06:42.729
My mother, “If you're right, you're right.

166
00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:45.024
If you're wrong, you're wrong.”

167
00:06:45.024 --> 00:06:50.988
Out of nine boys, two of them was in trouble all the time.

168
00:06:50.988 --> 00:06:54.200
My mother never went to the jailhouse.

169
00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.494
She never took incoming calls.

170
00:06:56.494 --> 00:06:59.706
She never put money on their books.

171
00:06:59.706 --> 00:07:02.000
<v DIGILORMO>She said, "Deal with it."

172
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.588
<v PAYNE>“Deal with it, because I told you not to steal.

173
00:07:06.588 --> 00:07:09.800
I told you not to do drugs.

174
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:13.929
And those two things don't call me for anything.”

175
00:07:13.929 --> 00:07:18.059
She still got her . . . She gave us tough love.

176
00:07:18.059 --> 00:07:21.271
And she taught us how to work.

177
00:07:21.271 --> 00:07:26.318
If you don't have it, wait until you can get it.

178
00:07:26.318 --> 00:07:29.529
And if you pick up a bad

179
00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:32.741
<c.p>habit, know that you could afford it.

180
00:07:32.741 --> 00:07:40.082
You better not . . . If you start smoking, you better not go ask anyone for a cigarette.

181
00:07:40.082 --> 00:07:41.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

182
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.871
<v PAYNE>Because if that's your bad habit, then you support it.

183
00:07:44.871 --> 00:07:47.194
<v DIGILORMO>She sounds like a strong woman.

184
00:07:47.194 --> 00:07:49.129
<v PAYNE>She was a strong woman.

185
00:07:49.129 --> 00:07:49.516
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

186
00:07:49.516 --> 00:07:52.613
<v PAYNE>She made us go to church every Sunday.

187
00:07:52.613 --> 00:07:55.710
I don't care if we slept in church.

188
00:07:55.710 --> 00:08:04.226
I don't care what you had to do, but she pulled you by your ears and you going to church every Sunday.

189
00:08:04.226 --> 00:08:05.000
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

190
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.326
<v PAYNE>We was Catholic.

191
00:08:06.326 --> 00:08:08.977
You go to catechism every day.

192
00:08:08.977 --> 00:08:10.744
You did your confirmation.

193
00:08:10.744 --> 00:08:12.953
You did your first communion.

194
00:08:12.953 --> 00:08:14.279
You did everything.

195
00:08:14.279 --> 00:08:16.047
She believed in her . . .

196
00:08:16.047 --> 00:08:17.814
<v DIGILORMO>She’s a stanch Catholic.

197
00:08:17.814 --> 00:08:18.698
<v PAYNE>Very much.

198
00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:19.140
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

199
00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.465
<v PAYNE>Very strong woman.

200
00:08:20.465 --> 00:08:20.907
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

201
00:08:20.907 --> 00:08:22.233
<v PAYNE>Very respectable woman.

202
00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:24.000
<v DIGILORMO>Sounds like it.

203
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:31.364
<v PAYNE>Because all the years—that might be restricted too—that my mother raised us as a single woman with thirteen kids,

204
00:08:31.364 --> 00:08:36.403
my mother never had a man . . . another man to come in our home.

205
00:08:36.403 --> 00:08:39.504
We’ve never seen our mother with anyone else.

206
00:08:39.504 --> 00:08:44.543
And just like she said, "To get respect you have to give it."

207
00:08:44.543 --> 00:08:44.930
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

208
00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.868
<v PAYNE>You have to earn it.

209
00:08:46.868 --> 00:08:48.419
And she did that.

210
00:08:48.419 --> 00:08:51.907
And she taught us to do the same thing.

211
00:08:51.907 --> 00:08:52.295
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

212
00:08:52.295 --> 00:08:53.070
That's amazing.

213
00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:53.845
I'm jealous.

214
00:08:53.845 --> 00:08:57.333
I need to grow up in that house. [laughs]

215
00:08:57.333 --> 00:09:00.822
<v PAYNE>I mean, and we had to love each other.

216
00:09:00.822 --> 00:09:04.310
I don't care if we fought among each other.

217
00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:08.574
Before you go to bed you going to kiss each other.

218
00:09:08.574 --> 00:09:10.899
You going to hug each other.

219
00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:13.225
You going to say you’re sorry.

220
00:09:13.225 --> 00:09:14.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

221
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.143
<v PAYNE>And you going to make it start all over the next day.

222
00:09:19.143 --> 00:09:19.571
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

223
00:09:19.571 --> 00:09:24.286
<v PAYNE>And you said prayers when you got up in the morning.

224
00:09:24.286 --> 00:09:27.286
You said prayers . . . on your knees prayers.

225
00:09:27.286 --> 00:09:29.000
<v DIGILORMO>Like by the bed?

226
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:29.429
<v PAYNE>Yes.

227
00:09:29.429 --> 00:09:30.714
All of us.

228
00:09:30.714 --> 00:09:36.286
All us little rug rat children. [laughs] And we didn't want for nothing.

229
00:09:36.286 --> 00:09:39.286
We had everything in life we needed.

230
00:09:39.286 --> 00:09:39.714
Everything.

231
00:09:39.714 --> 00:09:41.000
<v DIGILORMO>Sounds like paradise.

232
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:47.000
So what were some of your responsibilities as a kid around the house?

233
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.429
<v PAYNE>Oh we had chores.

234
00:09:48.429 --> 00:09:51.286
<v DIGILORMO>So as a baby, what . . . or you know,

235
00:09:51.286 --> 00:09:59.143
as the youngest did you get any sort of preferential treatment or were you given just as many chores as everybody else?

236
00:09:59.143 --> 00:10:01.643
<v PAYNE>Just as many chores as everybody else.

237
00:10:01.643 --> 00:10:06.643
It was so many of us to wash dishes that she had a list.

238
00:10:06.643 --> 00:10:15.214
It was a weekly planner on the wall that was rolled out with everybody’s name by what you had to do for the day.

239
00:10:15.214 --> 00:10:15.571
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

240
00:10:15.571 --> 00:10:21.286
<v PAYNE>And by the time I come along I guess chores wasn't that really important to me . . .

241
00:10:21.286 --> 00:10:22.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

242
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.000
<v PAYNE>. . . because my brothers spoiled me.

243
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.000
<v DIGILORMO>What you mean by that?

244
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.200
So they just took up slack, or what?

245
00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.800
<v PAYNE>They took up slack.

246
00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.000
They protect me.

247
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.400
They gave me what I wanted.

248
00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.000
I mean, they just gave me anything I wanted.

249
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.600
My mom gave me what I wanted, but I learned at an early age.

250
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.000
I started working when I was like in the ninth grade.

251
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:48.400
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

252
00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.200
<v PAYNE>Because my mom was putting a couple brothers through college . . . older brothers.

253
00:10:53.200 --> 00:11:01.200
So by the time I got to the ninth grade, I didn't want my mom to do it no more.

254
00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.200
So I worked and I paid for all my graduation.

255
00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:08.000
I took care of all my clothes.

256
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.800
I paid a bill in the house.

257
00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:20.400
Just about everybody else was gone so I didn't want my mom to have the strain to worry about sending one brother to college.

258
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.400
Making sure he has everything.

259
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.800
And she has . . . She went beyond.

260
00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.800
So I just became very independent at a young age.

261
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:29.200
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

262
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.000
<v PAYNE>And started taking care of myself.

263
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.977
<v DIGILORMO>And also your family a little bit too?

264
00:11:34.977 --> 00:11:35.349
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

265
00:11:35.349 --> 00:11:35.721
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

266
00:11:35.721 --> 00:11:36.465
<v PAYNE>Yeah, that . . .

267
00:11:36.465 --> 00:11:36.837
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

268
00:11:36.837 --> 00:11:37.209
Impressive.

269
00:11:37.209 --> 00:11:43.163
<v PAYNE>That way my mom could have made sure that my brother that was going to college,

270
00:11:43.163 --> 00:11:48.744
and out of thirteen I think he was the second one to go to college.

271
00:11:48.744 --> 00:11:50.233
So she was really

272
00:11:50.233 --> 00:11:55.442
<c.p>trying to make sure that if you wanted an education you could get it.

273
00:11:55.442 --> 00:11:57.302
And that one chose to.

274
00:11:57.302 --> 00:12:04.000
So I chose to let her take care of that while I would take care of myself.

275
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:04.484
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

276
00:12:04.484 --> 00:12:05.938
That's pretty admirable.

277
00:12:05.938 --> 00:12:11.266
Especially at such a . . . What are you, fourteen in ninth grade?

278
00:12:11.266 --> 00:12:12.719
Something like that.

279
00:12:12.719 --> 00:12:13.203
Thirteen?

280
00:12:13.203 --> 00:12:13.688
Fourteen?

281
00:12:13.688 --> 00:12:14.172
Wow.

282
00:12:14.172 --> 00:12:18.531
<v PAYNE>I had a driver's license at fourteen years old.

283
00:12:18.531 --> 00:12:19.500
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay.

284
00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.438
<v PAYNE>So I was rolling.

285
00:12:21.438 --> 00:12:23.375
<v DIGILORMO>[laughs] I got you.

286
00:12:23.375 --> 00:12:25.797
It was a different day.

287
00:12:25.797 --> 00:12:35.000
Alright well, so you said before in the interview with your brothers that your family had a garden?

288
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.888
<v PAYNE>Yeah, right in the backyard back there.

289
00:12:37.888 --> 00:12:38.300
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

290
00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.362
<v PAYNE>This is my grandparent's home.

291
00:12:40.362 --> 00:12:40.775
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

292
00:12:40.775 --> 00:12:46.550
<v PAYNE>From the time they moved from Carencro [Louisiana] to here, this was their house.

293
00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:46.962
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

294
00:12:46.962 --> 00:12:52.738
<v PAYNE>This is where all the kids lived with my grandmother, because she was blind.

295
00:12:52.738 --> 00:12:57.275
And in the backyard they had a hog pen and everything.

296
00:12:57.275 --> 00:13:03.875
And they killed the pigs in the back yard and butchered and did the blood boudin.

297
00:13:03.875 --> 00:13:04.700
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah right.

298
00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:07.175
<v PAYNE>And hog cracklin’ and all that.

299
00:13:07.175 --> 00:13:08.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

300
00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.406
<v PAYNE>Yeah, right in this yard.

301
00:13:09.406 --> 00:13:11.094
<v DIGILORMO>Would they hunt for those hogs?

302
00:13:11.094 --> 00:13:11.938
<v PAYNE>They grew them.

303
00:13:11.938 --> 00:13:12.781
They raised them.

304
00:13:12.781 --> 00:13:13.625
<v DIGILORMO>They raised them?

305
00:13:13.625 --> 00:13:13.906
Okay.

306
00:13:13.906 --> 00:13:15.031
<v PAYNE>We had hog pens.

307
00:13:15.031 --> 00:13:15.312
<v DIGILORMO>Alright.

308
00:13:15.312 --> 00:13:19.812
<v PAYNE>You put on some big old rubber boots and you go slop the pigs, you know.

309
00:13:19.812 --> 00:13:20.094
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

310
00:13:20.094 --> 00:13:23.469
Man I didn't know if they had like wild hogs around here.

311
00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:25.438
<v PAYNE>No. They would get them . . . domestic pigs.

312
00:13:25.438 --> 00:13:26.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

313
00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:33.090
<v PAYNE>Raise them until they get so big and as soon as it turn cool that's when they butcher them.

314
00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.955
Not when it's hot weather.

315
00:13:34.955 --> 00:13:35.328
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

316
00:13:35.328 --> 00:13:37.940
<v PAYNE>And they would do it right there.

317
00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:46.896
They had a fenced in hog pen with pigs and we would go slop the pigs and chase them and run in the mud.

318
00:13:46.896 --> 00:13:47.642
<v DIGILORMO>That's funny.

319
00:13:47.642 --> 00:13:51.373
I bet it was fun though when you were little.

320
00:13:51.373 --> 00:13:56.224
<v PAYNE>Yeah, and then my grandmother . . . We didn't have bathrooms in the house back

321
00:13:56.224 --> 00:13:56.597
<c.p>then.

322
00:13:56.597 --> 00:14:01.075
My grandmother had an outside toilet with two toilet seats in it.

323
00:14:01.075 --> 00:14:01.448
<v DIGILORMO>Alright.

324
00:14:01.448 --> 00:14:04.060
<v PAYNE>The cleanest toilet in the whole neighborhood.

325
00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:07.791
We wouldn't even use our outside toilet at our house.

326
00:14:07.791 --> 00:14:15.254
We come to my grandmother's house to use her outside toilet because it was so clean with double toilet seats.

327
00:14:15.254 --> 00:14:16.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

328
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.328
<v PAYNE>And nobody had no wall between it.

329
00:14:19.328 --> 00:14:25.033
You could sit there and hold hands in the wooden toilet outside.

330
00:14:25.033 --> 00:14:25.984
<v DIGILORMO>That's funny.

331
00:14:25.984 --> 00:14:27.410
<v PAYNE>I always wondered.

332
00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:32.639
I was like, "How my grandmother keep this toilet so clean?

333
00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:35.016
Because it's just a hole."

334
00:14:35.016 --> 00:14:40.721
And you would have maggots and all that stuff growing in it.

335
00:14:40.721 --> 00:14:41.197
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

336
00:14:41.197 --> 00:14:45.475
<v PAYNE>Everybody else’s toilet had all that stuff growing it.

337
00:14:45.475 --> 00:14:46.902
Not my grandmother's.

338
00:14:46.902 --> 00:14:47.377
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

339
00:14:47.377 --> 00:14:50.230
<v PAYNE>And I was telling my sister.

340
00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:56.410
She said, “Well I wonder if hers flushed . . . to keep it so clean.”

341
00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:58.787
<v DIGILORMO>Oh that would be funny.

342
00:14:58.787 --> 00:15:00.213
She's fooling everybody.

343
00:15:00.213 --> 00:15:04.016
She got running water and everything out there.

344
00:15:04.016 --> 00:15:04.967
Oh man.

345
00:15:04.967 --> 00:15:06.393
So let's see.

346
00:15:06.393 --> 00:15:10.672
I asked you if your family had a garden.

347
00:15:10.672 --> 00:15:14.000
Do you remember what they grew?

348
00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.000
<v PAYNE>Sugar cane.

349
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:15.500
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

350
00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:25.500
<v PAYNE>I remember we couldn't wait until sugar cane came out so we could cut it down and chew on it.

351
00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:26.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

352
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.500
<v PAYNE>And sit out there and eat sugarcane.

353
00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:34.500
And they grew cabbage, mustard greens, green beans, tomatoes, cucumbers . . .

354
00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:38.000
<v DIGILORMO>Just about anything that would grow.

355
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:38.321
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

356
00:15:38.321 --> 00:15:38.641
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

357
00:15:38.641 --> 00:15:42.808
And so that made up a lot of y’all’s diet then I guess?

358
00:15:42.808 --> 00:15:46.654
Or a lot of the food that was cooked in the house?

359
00:15:46.654 --> 00:15:47.615
<v PAYNE>Yeah, and chickens.

360
00:15:47.615 --> 00:15:47.936
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

361
00:15:47.936 --> 00:15:49.538
<v PAYNE>I didn't like yard chickens.

362
00:15:49.538 --> 00:15:53.385
<v DIGILORMO>I was about to say that . . . you didn’t like yard birds? [laughs]

363
00:15:53.385 --> 00:15:57.551
<v PAYNE>They say a chicken runs like a chicken with its head cut off.

364
00:15:57.551 --> 00:16:03.000
You wring a chicken neck that chicken going to run around in the yard for minutes.

365
00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:03.372
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

366
00:16:03.372 --> 00:16:05.233
And that freaks you out?

367
00:16:05.233 --> 00:16:06.349
<v PAYNE>With no head.

368
00:16:06.349 --> 00:16:10.070
Then you have to take it and boil it and . . .

369
00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:10.814
<v DIGILORMO>Pluck it?

370
00:16:10.814 --> 00:16:11.930
<v PAYNE>. . . pluck the feathers.

371
00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:12.302
Yeah.

372
00:16:12.302 --> 00:16:16.023
<v DIGILORMO>So it's not that you don't like to eat it.

373
00:16:16.023 --> 00:16:19.000
You don't like to fool with them?

374
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:20.037
<v PAYNE>And I didn't like . . .

375
00:16:20.037 --> 00:16:21.852
<v DIGILORMO>Or you didn't like to eat it?

376
00:16:21.852 --> 00:16:23.667
<v PAYNE>I didn't like the taste of them.

377
00:16:23.667 --> 00:16:24.185
<v DIGILORMO>Oh really?

378
00:16:24.185 --> 00:16:24.444
Okay.

379
00:16:24.444 --> 00:16:28.852
<v PAYNE>And they would put them up in the pen for so many days and feed them corn.

380
00:16:28.852 --> 00:16:30.148
And get it cleaned out.

381
00:16:30.148 --> 00:16:33.000
But I still didn't like the taste of yard chicken.

382
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:33.507
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

383
00:16:33.507 --> 00:16:34.521
That's funny.

384
00:16:34.521 --> 00:16:36.042
So let's see.

385
00:16:36.042 --> 00:16:38.577
What about anything like fish?

386
00:16:38.577 --> 00:16:43.141
Did y'all eat fish when you were growing up?

387
00:16:43.141 --> 00:16:43.648
<v PAYNE>Crabs.

388
00:16:43.648 --> 00:16:44.155
<v DIGILORMO>Crabs.

389
00:16:44.155 --> 00:16:45.169
Oh yeah.

390
00:16:45.169 --> 00:16:50.746
<v PAYNE>Crabs and it's amazing now but we ate crabs every Saturday.

391
00:16:50.746 --> 00:16:51.761
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay.

392
00:16:51.761 --> 00:16:58.352
<v PAYNE>When most of the time, summer time and stuff, we always had crabs.

393
00:16:58.352 --> 00:17:00.380
<v DIGILORMO>Big crab boils or . . .?

394
00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:01.901
<v PAYNE>I don't remember.

395
00:17:01.901 --> 00:17:03.423
I don't remember.

396
00:17:03.423 --> 00:17:09.000
I guess my . . . I guess they went and caught them.

397
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.263
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah, probably so.

398
00:17:10.263 --> 00:17:14.474
<v PAYNE>And we always . . . That was one of the biggest things.

399
00:17:14.474 --> 00:17:17.000
Crawfish wasn't a thing back then.

400
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.211
When you have a boil we would have boiled crabs.

401
00:17:21.211 --> 00:17:24.158
We ate a lot of boiled crabs.

402
00:17:24.158 --> 00:17:24.579
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

403
00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:25.842
They're good too.

404
00:17:25.842 --> 00:17:27.947
<v PAYNE>I'd rather crabs than crawfish.

405
00:17:27.947 --> 00:17:28.368
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

406
00:17:28.368 --> 00:17:30.053
I'm getting that way.

407
00:17:30.053 --> 00:17:33.000
<v PAYNE>I don't like crawfish that much.

408
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:35.167
<v DIGILORMO>It's a lot of hard work.

409
00:17:35.167 --> 00:17:38.417
Of course, some people think crab's too much work.

410
00:17:38.417 --> 00:17:40.583
But you get that technique down . . .

411
00:17:40.583 --> 00:17:41.667
<v PAYNE>I'd rather crabs.

412
00:17:41.667 --> 00:17:42.028
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

413
00:17:42.028 --> 00:17:44.194
<v PAYNE>I like the taste of it.

414
00:17:44.194 --> 00:17:44.556
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

415
00:17:44.556 --> 00:17:46.000
<v PAYNE>Crawfish is gritty.

416
00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:46.413
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

417
00:17:46.413 --> 00:17:50.545
Crabs have that naturally kind of buttery taste to them.

418
00:17:50.545 --> 00:17:54.678
<v PAYNE>And I remember when my dad he cooked so much.

419
00:17:54.678 --> 00:17:57.983
My dad was the cooker of the family.

420
00:17:57.983 --> 00:17:58.397
<v DIGILORMO>Really?

421
00:17:58.397 --> 00:18:02.529
<v PAYNE>Yes and he used to make crawfish I guess coubion.

422
00:18:02.529 --> 00:18:04.595
With live crawfish in there.

423
00:18:04.595 --> 00:18:10.380
Break the tails and squeeze all that yellow stuff and really make some crawfish.

424
00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:16.992
And before he died my dad taught me how to barbeque, how to light a pit.

425
00:18:16.992 --> 00:18:21.537
He say, "Girl you going to have to know these things."

426
00:18:21.537 --> 00:18:21.950
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

427
00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:25.256
<v PAYNE>He taught me how to light a pit.

428
00:18:25.256 --> 00:18:28.149
He taught me how to boil crawfish.

429
00:18:28.149 --> 00:18:29.388
He taught me

430
00:18:29.388 --> 00:18:31.041
<c.p>how to make gumbo.

431
00:18:31.041 --> 00:18:35.174
He taught me all the things I needed to know.

432
00:18:35.174 --> 00:18:36.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

433
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.465
<v PAYNE>My dad made sure before he died . . .

434
00:18:38.465 --> 00:18:38.817
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

435
00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:41.634
<v PAYNE>. . . that I knew how to do all that.

436
00:18:41.634 --> 00:18:51.141
<v DIGILORMO>So you didn't . . . So your mom cooked but she didn't . . . I guess she had so much going on that she wanted you out the kitchen or something?

437
00:18:51.141 --> 00:18:54.662
<v PAYNE>No. She would make me get in the kitchen but . . .

438
00:18:54.662 --> 00:18:55.366
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay.

439
00:18:55.366 --> 00:19:00.296
<v PAYNE>I wasn't the one . . . When my mom was teaching me I was still young.

440
00:19:00.296 --> 00:19:01.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

441
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.562
<v PAYNE>So I wasn't interested.

442
00:19:02.562 --> 00:19:09.594
By the time my dad got sick I had been married, divorced, and was interested in the kitchen.

443
00:19:09.594 --> 00:19:09.984
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

444
00:19:09.984 --> 00:19:10.375
Right.

445
00:19:10.375 --> 00:19:12.328
<v PAYNE>But as a teenager, no.

446
00:19:12.328 --> 00:19:12.719
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

447
00:19:12.719 --> 00:19:15.844
<v PAYNE>The kitchen wasn't . . . I knew how to cook.

448
00:19:15.844 --> 00:19:19.359
She made sure that you had to know how.

449
00:19:19.359 --> 00:19:23.266
So I stayed in there long enough to know how.

450
00:19:23.266 --> 00:19:26.000
But I wasn't staying in there.

451
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:26.379
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

452
00:19:26.379 --> 00:19:26.758
Right.

453
00:19:26.758 --> 00:19:27.136
<v PAYNE>No.

454
00:19:27.136 --> 00:19:27.515
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

455
00:19:27.515 --> 00:19:29.030
<v PAYNE>I was more tomboy.

456
00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:35.470
I'd rather go down the street, fight with some boys, and all that good old stuff. [laughs]

457
00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:36.606
<v DIGILORMO>I hear that.

458
00:19:36.606 --> 00:19:39.636
<v PAYNE>I didn't want to do that kitchen thing.

459
00:19:39.636 --> 00:19:42.667
I didn't want to do that girl thing.

460
00:19:42.667 --> 00:19:44.182
That little dress thing.

461
00:19:44.182 --> 00:19:48.348
That little girly, frilly . . . I didn't do none of that stuff.

462
00:19:48.348 --> 00:19:48.727
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

463
00:19:48.727 --> 00:19:49.106
Yeah.

464
00:19:49.106 --> 00:19:51.000
<v PAYNE>That wasn't my thing.

465
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.586
<v DIGILORMO>I understand that.

466
00:19:52.586 --> 00:19:56.814
So when . . . You started school at Mossville, right?

467
00:19:56.814 --> 00:19:57.343
<v PAYNE>Yes.

468
00:19:57.343 --> 00:20:05.800
<v DIGILORMO>But that was after the high school . . . Or they had stopped doing grades nine through twelve?

469
00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:07.386
Is that right?

470
00:20:07.386 --> 00:20:10.557
<v PAYNE>It started form one to twelve.

471
00:20:10.557 --> 00:20:17.429
Then, 1969 was the last class of the twelfth grade year at Mossville.

472
